@@ -0,0 +1,456 @@
+# Crabcraft breaking: toprock, a six-step and two freezes
+
+A breaking set is toprock, footwork, power moves and freezes. The four tasks here are
+the parts of one for "World of Crabcraft" that a policy can be trained to track
+against a reference, the way the six `jumper.skill_*` moves are
+([`CRABCRAFT_SKILLS.md`](CRABCRAFT_SKILLS.md)): the dance machinery
+(`tasks/jumper/common/dance/env.py`), 50 Hz, all 22 joints, the tracking rewards, every
+std and threshold recomputed from its own clip in its `env_cfg.py`. The power moves are
+reward-driven and are not here ([`CRABCRAFT_POWER.md`](CRABCRAFT_POWER.md)).
+
+All four are trained, exported to `tasks/jumper/break_*/out/crabcraft` and in the
+`crabcraft` bundle ("The crabcraft bundle", below). Three passed the acceptance check
+written before their runs finished; the six-step did not, 14 degrees short of a full
+turn, and ships as it is ("The six-step").
+
+| task | the move |
+|---|---|
+| `jumper.break_toprock` | an 8-count at 107.1 beats a minute: the body bouncing on the beats, a jab with each claw, side steps with the pincers snapping, loopable |
+| `jumper.break_sixstep` | footwork: the body low and wide turns once round the point between its claws in 3.64 s, the walking legs running round it |
+| `jumper.break_clawstand` | a freeze: nose 34 degrees down on both claws and the middle feet, the rear legs up and out, hit on a beat and held 1.68 s |
+| `jumper.break_oneclaw` | the same freeze on one claw, the right one raised high and open |
+
+The clips are committed in each task's `media/` and were written by
+[`tools/author_breaking_clips.py`](../tools/author_breaking_clips.py), which builds on
+`tools/author_skill_clips.py` and whose docstring says how each move was made and why:
+
+```bash
+python tools/author_breaking_clips.py                     # all four
+python tools/author_breaking_clips.py --check --dry-run   # every check, physics included; writes nothing
+python tools/author_breaking_clips.py --survey            # the two freezes not kept
+```
+
+## The moves
+
+**Toprock**, 0.56 s a beat (107.1 bpm). One beat of count-in from a still `HOME`, then
+an 8-count. The body bounces 11 mm down and nods 2.3 degrees on every beat and comes up
+on every "and". Counts 1-4: the left claw leaves the floor on 1, is chambered by the
+visor on the "and", jabs forward on 2 with the pincer snapping shut and the shell
+twisting 8 degrees into it, and is back on the floor on 3; the right claw the same from
+3 to 5. Counts 5-8: the left middle foot steps 30 mm out and back in, then the right,
+the shell swaying 10 mm and 4 degrees onto each, the pincers open on the "and"s and
+shut on the beats. Count 1 and the 1 after count 8 are the same still pose, so the game
+can loop the bar.
+
+**Six-step.** The claws are the pivot: the body turns anticlockwise about the point
+between them, its origin travelling round a 185 mm circle, while the walking legs run
+round in two diagonal pairs, (LM, RR) and then (RM, LR), and each claw steps once a
+cycle in the middle of a pair's step, the right claw with the first pair and the left
+with the second (the widest triangle left under the centre of mass when a claw and a
+pair are up together). The walking feet stand 35 mm further out than at `HOME` and
+15 mm along their arcs, away from the way the turn folds the middle knees; the body is
+10 mm low. 30 degrees a cycle, a cycle 0.28 s.
+
+The claws step because they cannot stay planted the whole way round. Over two planted
+claws, 10 mm low, the body turns 60 degrees either way inside the soft joint limits; at
+70 the left shoulder (`LF_J0`) is at its soft limit, and from 80 to 140 degrees the
+claws cannot reach their spots at all.
+
+**Claw stand.** Two bounces on the beat while the body moves 50 mm towards the claws, so
+that the centre of mass is well inside the claws and the middle feet before anything
+leaves the floor; the rear feet lift straight up on the third beat; on the fourth the
+nose is 34 degrees down and the rear legs are up and out, each foot 160 mm up and 60 mm
+out from where it stood; held three beats; back the same way over two beats. **One
+claw**: the same, the right claw rising high and open with the rear legs, so that the
+freeze stands on three points, the left claw and the middle feet. It lands 0.36 s after
+the rear feet, when the nose has come up far enough: landing with them takes its wrist
+0.033 rad past its soft limit.
+
+## The game's windows
+
+As for the skills, the moments are in each clip's `events` (JSON, seconds into the
+clip), and the clip begins at `HOME` and ends with a lead-out back to it and 0.5 s
+standing there.
+
+| task | clip | window to play | moments |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| toprock | 6.24 s | 0.56 to 5.04, loopable | `beat1` .. `beat8` at 0.56, 1.12, 1.68, 2.24, 2.80, 3.36, 3.92, 4.48; `loop_from` 0.56, `loop_to` 5.04; `jab_left` 1.12, `jab_right` 2.24 |
+| sixstep | 5.32 s | 0.20 to 3.84 | `turn_from` 0.20, `turn_to` 3.84; steady from 0.48 (`steady_from`) to 3.56 (`steady_to`) |
+| clawstand | 6.52 s | 1.68 to 5.60 | `bounce1` 0.56, `bounce2` 1.12, `lift` 1.68, `hit` 2.24, `release` 3.92, `down` 4.48, `recovered` 5.60 |
+| oneclaw | 6.52 s | 1.68 to 5.60 | as the claw stand, and `claw_down` 4.84 |
+
+The toprock's file also carries `bpm` (107.14) and `beat_times_sim` (the nine beats), the
+source schema's own names for them. The freezes' bounces are on the same 0.56 s beat.
+
+## Feasibility, per clip
+
+All measured by `tools/author_breaking_clips.py --check` on this model (Apple M4 Pro,
+macOS, 2026-10-15). Each line is what `author_skill_clips.py`'s checks measure (its
+docstring has how), plus two this tool adds: the centre of pressure the motion needs,
+from the same inverse dynamics, and its distance inside the support polygon (the hull of
+every point on the floor); and `motion.py`'s check that the four walking feet are in one
+plane on the median row, which refuses a clip past 5 mm and counts a lifted foot.
+
+| | toprock | sixstep | clawstand | oneclaw |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| clip (move + lead-out + hold), s | 6.24 (5.32 + 0.40 + 0.50) | 5.32 (4.40 + 0.40 + 0.50) | 6.52 (5.60 + 0.40 + 0.50) | 6.52 (5.60 + 0.40 + 0.50) |
+| one turn, s | | 3.64 (3.36 at the steady rate) | | |
+| base height, mm | 95.5 to 108.5 | 96.5 to 106.5 | 100.5 to 108.5 | 100.5 to 108.5 |
+| tilt at most, deg | 4.6 | 0.0 (turns 360) | 34.4 | 34.4 |
+| least torque, peak N*m (continuous 1.2, plateau 1.7464) | 0.421 | 1.458 | 0.377 | 0.659 |
+| winding at most (1.0 = derate to 1.2 N*m) | 0.07 | 0.29 | 0.07 | 0.21 |
+| base residual at most, N (limit 0.5) | 0.000 | 0.317 | 0.000 | 0.000 |
+| centre of pressure inside the support, least, mm | 101.3 | 76.1 | 56.5 | 51.1 |
+| hard joint margin, rad | 0.100 | 0.100 | 0.100 | 0.100 |
+| soft joint margin, rad | +0.015 | +0.015 | +0.014 (`RF_J3`) | +0.015 |
+| fastest joint, rad/s (corner 30.7) | 6.4 | 29.9 (`RR_J2`) | 5.1 | 7.4 |
+| lowest foot pad / other claw hull / body hull, mm | -0.02 / +2.16 / +7.01 | -0.02 / +2.31 / +6.81 | -0.02 / +2.17 / +3.83 | -0.02 / +2.17 / +3.83 |
+| claw to chassis, mm | 26.3 | 26.5 | 26.2 | 17.0 |
+| claw to claw, mm | 10.3 | over 50 | over 50 | over 50 |
+| leg to leg, mm | 19.0 | 20.2 | 22.0 | 22.0 |
+| support feet out of plane, median row, mm (limit 5) | 0.29 | 4.54 | 0.57 | 0.57 |
+| solved vs keyed: x/y, z, pitch | 0.76 mm, 1.69 mm, 0.59 deg | 0.60, 1.56, 0.35 | 3.18, 2.26, 1.88 | 3.10, 2.40, 1.84 |
+
+The hard margin is the grippers' everywhere, as in the skills; nothing passes a soft
+limit. The least torque passes the servo's continuous rating only in the six-step, by
+0.26 N*m at the peak, and its winding stays at 0.29 of the derate. The freezes' solved
+pose differs from the keyed one by 1.8 to 1.9 degrees of pitch for the reason the
+skills' power-down did: the solve reads the feet at their pads' centres, and with the
+body 34 degrees nose-down the claws' pads stand on a point well off their centre.
+
+### The physics control
+
+The clip played on stiff position servos (kp 400, kd 1, 1 ms, the training collision
+model, base free), against its base pose. On the MJCF's own stiff contacts / on the
+training contacts:
+
+| | toprock | sixstep | clawstand | oneclaw |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| height error, median / max, mm | 0.10 / 0.24; 3.25 / 3.86 | 0.14 / 0.44; 2.83 / 5.16 | 0.12 / 0.34; 2.83 / 5.62 | 0.12 / 0.35; 2.84 / 5.86 |
+| tilt error, median / max, deg | 0.02 / 0.07; 0.25 / 0.64 | 0.02 / 0.11; 0.28 / 0.82 | 0.03 / 0.18; 0.25 / 1.71 | 0.03 / 0.17; 0.25 / 1.36 |
+| yaw error at most, deg | 0.15; 0.17 | 8.75; 6.00 | 0.07; 0.02 | 0.45; 0.16 |
+| drift at most, mm | 0.8; 1.8 | 28.3; 31.6 | 4.3; 2.2 | 4.2; 2.3 |
+| foot and claw floor depth, median / max, mm | 0.01 / 0.14; 3.70 / 6.09 | 0.08 / 0.38; 3.40 / 6.53 | 0.01 / 0.21; 3.39 / 6.97 | 0.01 / 0.27; 3.41 / 7.16 |
+| rows with leg-on-leg contact | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| joint error at most, rad | 0.003 | 0.006 | 0.004 | 0.004 |
+
+Standing at `HOME` the training contacts sink the feet 3.39 mm and the base 2.82 mm, the
+baseline every training-contact figure is read against.
+
+**The six-step creeps round.** Open loop, the turn ends 8.75 degrees further round than
+the clip on the stiff contacts and 6.0 on the training ones, while every joint stays
+within 0.006 rad of the reference: about 0.7 degrees a cycle, from the feet, not the
+joints. A planted foot is held by its pad's centre while the body turns 16.5 degrees
+over it (22.5 over a claw), and the point where the rounded pad meets the floor moves along it, 2.1 to 3.9 mm at
+the median stance for a walking foot and 8.7 mm for a claw. Planting the claws by that
+point instead was tried: the stiff-contact run ended 2.9 degrees off, the training one
+11.7, and the claw stand's claws crossed, so the clip keeps the skills' rule. (Written
+before training: "a tracking policy reads the base's pose in the world and corrects it
+with its feet". It does not. The actor is shown the tilt error and the gyro, not the
+base's position or heading, which the robot has nothing to measure with, so the turn a
+policy makes is open loop; see "Trained".) The six-step's stiff-servo torque peak, 34.6 N*m, is
+the control's damping against the reference's own joint speed (kd 1 at 30 rad/s), not
+a demand of the move; the others peak at 1.6 to 2.5 N*m.
+
+### The six-step's pace
+
+The fastest pace at which the base can be balanced on every row with the feet at
+friction 0.5 and every joint stays under the servo's corner speed, one turn at the steady
+rate (the clip adds one cycle for speeding up and slowing down):
+
+| cycle, s | one turn, s | base residual at most, N | fastest joint, rad/s | least torque, N*m |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.25 | 3.00 | 0.78 | 34.4 | 1.49 |
+| 0.26 | 3.12 | 0.61 | 31.2 | 1.45 |
+| 0.27 | 3.24 | 0.46 | 31.4 | 1.41 |
+| **0.28** | **3.36** | **0.32** | **29.9** | **1.46** |
+| 0.30 | 3.60 | 0.08 | 29.6 | 1.27 |
+| 0.32 | 3.84 | 0.00 | 25.2 | 1.27 |
+
+Balance alone allows 0.27 s, where the rear knees pass the corner speed, so the clip is
+at 0.28 s: 3.36 s a turn, 107 degrees a second. 0.32 s, `sixstep(cycle_s=0.32)`, is the
+pace with room on both should a policy not keep up. A policy was trained at both; the
+pace was not what held it back ("Trained"). The foot lift is 8 mm: at 10 mm the
+four walking feet are 5.5 mm out of plane on the median row and `motion.py` refuses the
+clip.
+
+### The freezes not kept
+
+`--survey` searches each over a grid of held poses.
+
+**Chair**: rolled onto the left side, held on the left claw and the left legs, the
+right claw and legs off the floor. With the right side up the support has to reach right
+of the centre of mass, so the left claw crosses under the front (100 to 140 mm) and the
+left feet step in under the body (0 to 60 mm), with the body rolled 14 to 26 degrees,
+-20 to +40 mm and 0 to 60 mm left. Of 216 poses 54 place every foot; none of them is
+inside the soft joint limits with nothing below the floor. The best-balanced of the 54
+has the centre of mass 25 mm inside, a joint 0.18 rad past its soft limit and a hull 3 mm
+into the floor.
+
+**Shoulder stand**: nose down until the shell rests on the claws' shoulders, the claws
+planted, all four walking feet off the floor. The centre of mass stays behind the line
+through the two shoulders up to 34 degrees nose down; it is 3 mm inside the support at
+40 degrees and 7 mm at 46, beside the deployment's 50-degree tilt fallback, and the best
+pose at each of those pitches has joints 0.12 to 0.18 rad past their soft limits.
+
+## Terminations
+
+None relaxed. The root height threshold is jumper.dance's 40 mm for all four, by its own
+rule (a threshold above the clip's whole vertical band: 13, 10, 8 and 8 mm). The tilt
+termination compares the robot's tilt with the reference's (as `1 - cos`), so the
+freezes' 34.4 degrees need no change: it fires past 58 degrees there, and the deployed
+controller's tilt fallback, 50 degrees absolute, is 15.6 degrees beyond the freeze. The
+support-foot termination is an error against the reference too, so the freezes' raised
+rear feet are not one. The one number not taken from jumper.dance before training is the
+six-step's power weight, -0.007: jumper.dance sizes its -0.02 to cost about 3% of the positive budget,
+and the six-step's 4.9 rad/s would make -0.02 cost 8.2%, a price on keeping up with the
+turn. Training changed three more for the six-step, its smoothness weights, the stds of
+the two global root terms and two terms in its critic ("Trained").
+
+## Training
+
+As first proposed, before any run. On a CUDA machine, as the skills were:
+
+```bash
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.break_toprock   --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 2000
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.break_sixstep   --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 3000
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.break_clawstand --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 2000
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.break_oneclaw   --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 3000
+```
+
+The budgets are suggestions, not measurements: the skills' 2000 and 3000. The six-step
+steps a foot or a claw every 0.14 s with its rear knees at the corner speed, the most
+contact change and the fastest joints of any clip here; the one-claw freeze balances on
+three points. Export with no performance video (there is no music):
+
+```bash
+python scripts/export.py --task jumper.break_sixstep --checkpoint logs/jumper/jumper.break_sixstep/<run>/model_2999.pt --no-video
+```
+
+### Checked here
+
+Each task trained for five iterations on `native:cpu` with 64 environments (Apple M4
+Pro), from a build that converts its clip through `motion.py`'s checks: joint limits,
+support-foot coplanarity and height, and the speed between control steps. All four
+built, stepped, logged finite rewards and saved `model_4.pt`:
+
+| task | mean reward, iteration 0 then 4 | mean episode length at 4, steps | s an iteration |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| toprock | -1.94, -9.98 | 53.7 | 0.62 to 0.74 |
+| sixstep | -2.16, -10.91 | 55.7 | 0.64 to 0.73 |
+| clawstand | -0.61, -3.49 | 19.4 | 0.62 to 0.64 |
+| oneclaw | -0.71, -4.19 | 23.2 | 0.60 to 0.64 |
+
+The freezes' episodes are the short ones, and the support-foot termination is why: it
+ends nearly every one of their episodes at this stage (`Episode_Termination/ee_body_pos`
+4.0 and 3.7 at iteration 4, 0.0 for the toprock and the six-step). An untrained policy
+started anywhere in the freeze lets the rear legs drop from the reference's 160 mm and
+passes 50 mm within a few steps. That is the termination doing its job on feet that go
+further from `HOME`'s than any skill's; if training stalls there, raising
+`support_foot_error` above the lift for these two is the lever, not taken here.
+
+`jumper.break_sixstep`'s `model_4.pt` exported cleanly: observation 209, action 22,
+every observed term deployable, the ONNX within 1.16e-06 of the torch policy, and
+`sixstep.motion.trajectory.json` beside it, 266 rows with the whole turn in its root
+quaternions.
+
+## Trained
+
