+
+**A: the walking legs at half size.** A policy trained on the clip as committed swung its
+rear knees through 9.3 and 7.8 degrees (RMS about their mean over the steady turn) where
+the clip swings them through 20.3 and 19.4, the hips' lift joints through about half
+too, and the body turned 325 of the 360 degrees. It had plateaued: 7.80 degrees of joint
+RMS at iteration 1200, 7.64 at 2100, 7.89 at 3999.
+
+**The reason is the smoothness terms, not the servo.** The servo's PD damps the joint's
+own speed (kd 0.5 against kp 10), so to hold the clip's speed a policy must lead the
+reference by kd * qd / kp, 1.5 rad at the rear knees' 29.9 rad/s. The actions that do
+that change by 15.3 a step (squared, summed over the 22 joints; the second difference
+21.0) through the move, where `jumper.dance`'s -0.1 and -0.05 charge 39.7% of the 6.5 a
+step a perfect policy earns. At 0.32 s a cycle it is 25.8%; for the toprock and the
+freezes, 0.2% and 0.1%. That is the price the power weight was already lowered for
+("Terminations", above), five times larger.
+
+**B, the slower pace, was the fallback this document named, and it was not enough:** 4.02
+degrees and 329 degrees turned. **C and D**, the smoothness weights scaled together until
+a perfect policy pays 3.2% (C: -0.008 and -0.004) or 3.1% (D: -0.012 and -0.006) of the
+budget, the share the power term's rule gives, brought the joints to 2.6 and 2.2 degrees
+and the motion to 0.96 and 0.97 of the clip's size, and the turn to 334 at either pace.
+The pace, then, was not what was wrong, and the clip stays at 0.28 s.
+
+**The turn is open loop.** The actor sees the reference, its own joints, the gyro and the
+tilt error; nothing tells it where the base is or which way it faces, because the robot
+has nothing to measure them with. So the turn it makes is what its feet do on the floor,
+and that depends on the floor: with randomisation, the five rollouts whose feet drew a
+friction of 1.34 to 1.44 turned 306 to 312 degrees in C, the four at 0.56 to 0.91
+turned 341 to 350, and the one at 1.21 turned 331 (without randomisation the friction is
+1.2). Every variant shows the same split. **E** tightened mjlab's two global root terms,
+whose humanoid stds (0.3 m, 0.4 rad) left a base 80 mm off the reference's path scoring 0.93
+and a turn 25 degrees short 0.30, to 0.05 m and 0.2 rad: 339 degrees. **F** also shows
+the critic, which is thrown away at export, the base's position and heading error
+against the reference (mjlab's `motion_anchor_pos_b` and `motion_anchor_ori_b`), so
+that it can price the bias the actor has to learn blind.
+
+F turns 345.9 degrees without randomisation and keeps the base within 44.2 mm of the
+reference's path: inside the 50 mm, 4 degrees outside the 10. Everything else passes.
+
+**The six-step is not accepted by that check, and it ships as it is.** On 2026-10-15 F
+was taken for "World of Crabcraft" with its turn short: exported into `out/crabcraft` like
+the other three (below) and bundled. Its configuration is what `env_cfg.py` holds, so that
+a next run starts from it. With randomisation its turn still follows the friction, 329 to 335
+degrees at 1.34 to 1.44 and 355 to 359 at 0.56 to 0.91; a policy that cannot see its
+heading can only learn the bias that suits the friction it trained on, and the critic's
+two terms moved that bias, they did not close the loop. What would close it is an
+observation: the reference's base angular velocity beside the gyro's, which the robot
+does measure, would show the actor a turn falling behind while it happens. That changes
+the observation (209 to 212) and the controller that assembles it, and is not tried
+here. Nor was a checkpoint picked by this check: F's turn read 348.1 at iteration
+2100, 342.9 at 3400 and 345.9 at 3999, with no trend among them to pick from, and a
+checkpoint chosen on the rollout it is then judged by would pass by selection.
+
