+# Crabcraft power moves: a spin, a jump spin and a back flip
+
+"World of Crabcraft" wants a routine that could win a breaking battle. The footwork,
+the toprock and the freezes are reference clips another branch tracks; this branch
+asks the simulator the other question -- which power moves can this robot make at
+all, and how far do they go -- with three reward-driven tasks and no recording:
+
+| task | the move | what it is paid for |
+|---|---|---|
+| `jumper.power_spin` | spin in place about the vertical axis, the crab's windmill | yaw rate, counter-clockwise from above |
+| `jumper.power_jumpspin` | jump, turn about the vertical axis in the air, land on the feet | the turn the flight made, latched at the landing |
+| `jumper.power_backflip` | jump, flip nose-up through a whole turn in the air, land on the feet | the pitch turn the flight made, latched at the landing |
+
+Everything below is simulation: MuJoCo on this repository's model, trained on
+`warp:cuda` and measured on `native:cpu`. Nothing here has run on a robot.
+
+"World of Crabcraft" ships three of the policies below, each exported against a task of
+its own ("Shipped"): the spin that keeps to its feet, `pw-spin-c20L`, as
+`jumper.power_spin_feet`; the double jump spin, `pw-jspin720-ho`, as
+`jumper.power_jumpspin720`; and the back flip, `pw-flip-brc`, as `jumper.power_backflip`.
+The two jumps were first trained, as `pw-jspin720` and `pw-flip-T`, from an episode
+start no controller gives a robot, and took off from nothing else ("Under the
+controller"); `pw-jspin720-ho` and `pw-flip-brc` are those policies trained on from the start
+the bundle's controller does give ("The handover start"). All three are modes of the `crabcraft`
+bundle, the flip's with the cascade's tilt fallback lifted for it alone ("In the bundle"), the
+spin's and the 720's with their joint targets let past the stops, as they trained ("The
+controller gap"). Both jumps then shook after landing, and the exports now come from runs
+that trained them on to stand still afterwards: `cm-720-g` for the 720 and `lg-flip` for
+the flip ("Settling after the landing"). "The final bundle" measures all three through the
+bundle that carries them.
+
+## What all three share
+
+**The servo is the hardware's.** `common/actuator.py`'s curve, unchanged: 1.7464 N*m
+plateau to the 30.7 rad/s corner, exponential decay above it, nothing at 64 rad/s, and
+a 0.3 s peak budget that derates to the 1.2 N*m continuous rating. No limit was
+raised. Every policy below reaches the plateau on every gait joint at some point
+(peak 1.746 N*m), and the joints run past the corner speed (peaks 40 to 59 rad/s),
+where the curve itself gives less torque.
+
+**200 Hz control at kp=20, absolute targets around `HOME` at action scale 0.5**, and
+the strided five-frame history (406 actor inputs): `jumper.ref_free_jump`'s setup. The
+actor sees the IMU (angular rate, gravity), the 20 joints' positions, speeds, targets
+and currents -- nothing a robot cannot measure, no clock and no command.
+
+**Counter-clockwise and nose-up only.** The spin and the jump spin train without the
+left-right mirror (a counter-clockwise turn mirrors to a clockwise one); a clockwise
+version would be the mirror image of these policies, and is not trained here. The flip
+keeps the mirror, as
+`ref_free_jump` does: a pitch turn is its own mirror image.
+
+**The two jumps start from `ref_free_jump`'s `model_5999`**, committed with that
+task's example export: its actor already jumps on nothing but its body. The
+jumps' configs are `ref_free_jump`'s with the recording's feedforward, its joint
+prior and their schedule taken out (they were zero for its last 3000 iterations
+anyway), the rotation counted (`power_jumpspin/mdp/commands.py`), and the run started
+from that actor with a fresh critic (`power_jumpspin/runner.py`). Measured on the same
+footing as everything below (native:cpu, the jump spin's environment, 32 envs, DR on),
+that warm start jumps 0.26 to 0.29 m (the lowest point of the robot, against `HOME`),
+flies 0.38 to 0.44 s, turns -27 to +18 degrees in the air, and lands 32 of 32.
+
+## How it was measured
+
+`tools/eval_power.py`, deterministic policy (the distribution's mean), every
+environment from `HOME`, one episode each, on `native:cpu`:
+
+- **DR off**: no base-CoM offset, no encoder bias, the feet at the model's nominal
+  friction 1.2, no pushes, no observation noise; 4 environments (identical but for the
+  spin's random heading).
+- **DR on**: the training's randomisation, pushes and actor observation noise; 32
+  environments, each its own draw.
+
+Spin: rev/s is the yaw turned between 2 s and the end of the 6 s episode, divided by
+the time; drift is the base's horizontal distance from its spawn. Jumps: the turn in
+the air is the integrated rate from the own flight's takeoff to its touchdown (all six
+feet under 1 N); an upright landing is a jump that landed and ended at `HOME` or at the
+time limit, not by a tilt or crash termination, with the tilt under 30 degrees 0.3 s
+after the touchdown.
+
+## jumper.power_spin
+
+### Reward and termination
+
+`spin_rate`, the base's world-frame yaw rate, 1 per rad/s, linear and signed -- turning
+back and forth earns nothing. Against it: upright (1.0, `exp(-|g_xy|^2 / 0.1)`),
+horizontal drift from the spawn (`-(d / 0.1 m)^2`), any non-foot part on the ground
+(-5 a step), legs touching each other (-5), joint limits (-1), and target chatter
+(`ref_free_jump`'s -0.2304 per squared radian). Episodes are 6 s from `HOME`, standing,
+facing a random way. Termination: time-out, and tilt past 50 degrees (the controller's
+own limit, unchanged).
+
+### Runs
+
+One H100 each, `warp:cuda`, 4096 environments, `~/crabcraft-train/power/train_power.sbatch`
+(2 CPUs, 24G). "Reward" is the job log's last `Mean reward`.
+
+| run | job | commit | iterations | wall | peak GPU memory | final mean reward |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `pw-spin-a` | 248721 | 93bc654 | 0 to 399 | 527 s | 9637 MiB | 64.34 |
+| `pw-spin-L` | 248797 | 93bc654 | 399 to 2998, resumed from `pw-spin-a`'s `model_399` | 3248 s | 9637 MiB | 104.34 |
+| `pw-spin-c20` | 248745 | 4af8af8 (`illegal_contact` -20) | 0 to 399 | 486 s | 9637 MiB | 56.37 |
+| `pw-spin-c20L` | 248837 | 4af8af8 | 399 to 2998, resumed from `pw-spin-c20`'s `model_399` | 3304 s | 9637 MiB | 68.99 |
+
+All four exited 0. The committed config is `pw-spin-L`'s (`illegal_contact` -5); 4af8af8
+is a branch, `exp/power-spin-contact20`, that changes that one weight, and
+`jumper.power_spin_feet` is that weight as a task.
+
+### Results
+
+rev/s is sustained, from 2 s to 6 s; "best 1 s" the fastest one-second window; drift the
+base's distance from its spawn at 6 s.
+
+| checkpoint | DR | envs | rev/s, mean (min to max) | best 1 s | drift at 6 s, median (max) | tilt max | falls |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `pw-spin-a` `model_399` | off | 4 | 2.54 (2.38 to 2.62) | 2.77 | 0.24 m (0.40) | 17.4 deg | 0 |
+| `pw-spin-a` `model_399` | on | 16 | 2.52 (2.25 to 2.70) | 2.87 | 0.20 m (0.36) | 18.6 deg | 0 |
+| **`pw-spin-L` `model_2998`** | off | 4 | **4.36** (4.30 to 4.43) | 4.59 | 0.36 m (0.38) | 32.0 deg | 0 |
+| **`pw-spin-L` `model_2998`** | on | 32 | **4.27** (3.75 to 4.52) | 4.66 | 0.27 m (1.02) | 40.9 deg | 0 |
+| `pw-spin-c20L` `model_2998` | off | 4 | 2.33 (2.31 to 2.37) | 2.44 | 0.09 m (0.13) | 9.0 deg | 0 |
+| `pw-spin-c20L` `model_2998` | on | 32 | 2.32 (2.25 to 2.44) | 2.50 | 0.09 m (0.28) | 11.9 deg | 0 |
+
+`pw-spin-L` turns 24 to 25 times in the 6 s episode, from standing. Every gait joint
+reaches the 1.746 N*m plateau; the fastest joint reaches 51 rad/s (the corner is 30.7,
+where the curve has fallen to 0.78 N*m). With DR on, 3 of 32 wander more than 0.5 m
+from their spawn over the 6 s, one 1.02 m.
+
+### What it leans on
+
+It pivots on its middle legs. `pw-spin-L`'s `model_2998`, 4 s after a 2 s spin-up
+(native:cpu, DR off), by share of the ground's normal force: left-middle foot 37%,
+right-middle foot 22%, the two middle calves 9% and 9%, the left claw's palm (not its
+pad) 9%, left-rear foot 7%. A non-foot part is on the ground over 1 N in 32% to 42% of
+steps (DR on, 32 envs); training charged `illegal_contact` -1.63 a second at its end,
+33% of steps -- paid, not avoided. `pw-spin-c20L`, at four times the charge, still
+touches in a median 14% of steps on native (DR on), 0.05 to 0.55 by environment.
+
+So the spin was measured again with the two contact assumptions that favour it
+changed (`tools/eval_power.py --foot-torsion`, `--nonfoot-friction`; DR off, 4 envs):
+
+| contacts | `pw-spin-L` rev/s | `pw-spin-c20L` rev/s |
+|---|---|---|
+| as trained: feet pivot freely (condim 3), calves and palms at 1.0 | 4.36 | 2.33 |
+| feet with torsional friction 0.005 (condim 4) | 4.40 | -- |
+| calves, palms and shell at friction 0.4 | 3.10 | 2.24 |
+| both | 3.47 | -- |
+
+No falls in any. Free pivoting costs nothing; the calves' and the palm's grip is 29%
+of `pw-spin-L`'s speed, and 4% of `pw-spin-c20L`'s. **3.1 rev/s is the figure to
+trust for `pw-spin-L` on a floor where its calves slip; 2.2 rev/s for the variant that
+keeps to its feet**, which also stays within 0.13 m of its spot (DR off) instead of
+0.38.
+
+## jumper.power_jumpspin
+
+### Reward and termination
+
+`ref_free_jump`'s rewards at their final weights -- the apex of the robot's lowest
+point (4 per 0.1 m, latched, paid after the landing), its height in flight (2),
+upright, `HOME` after the landing, drift, contact, collision, action rate -- plus:
+
+- `landed_turn`: the flight's yaw turn, latched at the touchdown and paid every step
+  after it, 4 per half turn, most for landing exactly a full turn round
+  (`cap - |turn - cap|`, the tent);
+- `flight_turn_rate`: the yaw rate while in the air, 1 per rad/s, until the flight has
+  turned a full turn.
+
+Terminations are `ref_free_jump`'s, unchanged: time-out, back at `HOME`, tilt past 50
+degrees.
+
+The tent and the cap on the rate came after the first long run. Without them
+(`landed_turn` clamped at a full turn, the rate paid without a cap) the jump spin went
