+
+**A whole back flip is within this robot's reach in simulation.** `pw-flip-T`'s
+DR-off rollout, read off the base's height and the gravity vector every 20 ms: it
+crouches 19 degrees nose-down, leaves the ground at about 0.145 s, is 90 degrees nose-up
+0.085 s later, upside down 0.14 s after takeoff with its base 0.31 m up, and round to
+level 0.225 s after takeoff. It carries on and touches down on its rear feet 0.285 s
+after takeoff, 48 degrees nose-up past the full turn, peaks at 413 degrees, and has
+rotated back to level 0.2 s later; 0.3 s after the touchdown it is under 12 degrees
+of tilt in every trial.
+
+What it does not do is land level. Over every trial it touches down 8 to 57 degrees
+nose-up (median 48) -- the turn has not stopped when the feet arrive, and the rear legs
+catch it. The tent paid less for that than for landing at 360 exactly, and 1500
+iterations of it moved the median from 58.6 to 47.8 degrees without removing it. The
+controller's 50 degree guard would have to be lifted for the flip's mode in any case
+(in the air it passes 178 degrees); a landing at 48 degrees is close to it.
+
+It is a short, violent move: 0.27 to 0.34 s in the air at a rise of 0.19 to 0.25 m,
+lower than the plain jump's 0.26 to 0.29 m because the push is spent on the turn as
+much as on the height. Every gait joint hits the 1.746 N*m plateau and the fastest
+reaches 53 rad/s.
