+## The handover start
+
+### What the controller hands over
+
+Measured through the bundle's own controller (crabcraft's `tools/motion/jumper_sim.py`
+`Host`, the `controller.so` a bundle carries, a mode switched into from standing): the
+switch-in ramp slides the setpoint to the mode's `HOME` at its own PD (`ramp_kp` 15,
+`ramp_kd` 0.8) and hands over once every joint is within `pose_reach_tol`, 0.10 rad, of
+it -- 0.57 s after the switch. At that tick the worst joint was 0.099 rad off `HOME` (the
+mean 0.029), the fastest moving at 0.20 rad/s, the torques 0.39 N*m at most (0.18 on
+average), and the policy's first observation held that one frame in all five slots of
+every history, its last action zero.
+
+### What changed
+
+`power_jumpspin/mdp/handover.py`, applied to both shipped jumps by
+`power_jumpspin.env_cfg.handover_start`. Every HOME spawn (the half of the episodes not
+spawned on the recording):
+
+- spawns with every gait joint up to `HANDOVER_POSE_RANGE`, 0.10 rad (the controller's
+  `pose_reach_tol`), off `HOME`, a reset event between `HOME` and the reference-state
+  spawns, which overwrite it for their share;
+- is held at `HOME` by `HandoverJointPositionAction` for a hold drawn from
+  `HANDOVER_HOLD_RANGE`, 0.02 to 0.6 s: the target at `HOME` and the raw action zero in
+  the action manager's own buffer, so the actor's `actions` and `action_rate_l2` read
+  zero, as on the robot. The hold is the jump command's go delay, which was zero
+  (`ref_free_jump`'s `GO_DELAY_RANGE`), so the command's own bookkeeping -- a takeoff
+  counts only after the go -- already means what it should;
+- has every observation history emptied at the go by `handover_history`, a step event, so
+  the frame the policy first acts on fills all five slots, as the controller's reset of
+  the history does.
+
+The episode grows by the longest hold, to 3.1 s. Replay (`play=True`) holds 0.5 s from
+`HOME` exactly. The actor's observation is unchanged, 406 wide, and so is the motor model;
+nothing in `rl/` or `tasks/jumper/common/` changed, and the bundle and controller need
+nothing new for it. `jumper.power_jumpspin`, which ships nothing, keeps its own start.
+
+The same moment in the environment (native:cpu, DR off, zero actions, 12 holds from
+0.08 to 0.47 s, the joints spawned up to 0.1 rad off): at the go every gait joint within
+0.021 rad of `HOME`, the fastest at 0.007 to 0.215 rad/s, the torques 0.37 to 0.42 N*m at
+most (0.14 to 0.20 on average), the base at 0.1011 to 0.1021 m against the controller's
+0.1017; the five frames identical and the last action zero. The joints settle under the
+policy's own kp 20 faster than under the ramp's 15, so the environment rarely hands over
+as far off `HOME` as the controller can.
+
+**The back flip needed two more changes.** Resumed on the handover start alone,
+`pw-flip-T` lost the flip before it found a takeoff, and the in-air rate it is paid
+(`flight_turn_rate`, 15 per rad/s, kept whatever the attempt did next) was farmed
+instead. `pw-flip-ho` ended paying 40.8 a second of it against 0.93 of `landed_turn`
+(`pw-flip-T`: 24.1 and 3.54), with tipped and crashed terminations `pw-flip-T` did not
+have, and from the handover its mean policy did not leave the ground (the table below).
+With crashes charged for (`pw-flip-hoc`) it took off 5 to 64 times an episode in hops
+under the 1 cm a flight needs to be the jump, rocking nose-up while its feet were off the
+floor, and was paid 25 to 167 for that against a landed flip's 94 -- without jumping
+once (`model_2950`, from the handover, DR on, 32 environments). So:
+
+- **`turn_clawback`** (`power_backflip/mdp/rewards.py`, weight -15): what
+  `flight_turn_rate` paid a flight is taken back on the step that flight is seen to end
+  without having become the jump, and everything still kept is taken back on the step a
+  failure ends the episode. The rate stays paid only for the one flight that is the jump,
+  and only for a flip still standing. On `pw-flip-ho`'s `model_4497` (from the reset, DR
+  on) every crashed episode nets 0; on `pw-flip-hoc`'s `model_2950` the hopping episodes
+  keep 0 to 9.7 of the 83 to 160 they were paid.
+- **A bridge from mjlab's reset** (`power_backflip/mdp/curriculum.py`): a share of the held
+  HOME spawns starts at mjlab's reset instead, where the flip still lands, so that what
+  the network does after the takeoff keeps being paid while it learns to take off from
+  `HOME`. `RotationJumpCommandCfg.reset_share`, 0.5 until iteration 2700 and down to 0 at
+  3400 (`BRIDGE_SHARE`, `BRIDGE_HOLD_ITERATION`, `BRIDGE_END_ITERATION`), on the run's own
+  iteration count, so 600 of the shipped run's 1500 iterations are the handover alone.
+
+Neither alone was enough. With the clawback and no bridge, `pw-flip-hot` jumped from the
+handover by iteration 2800 but turned 64 degrees there (32 of 32 landed, DR on) and 53 on
+average in training at 3150: a landed hop is paid and a partial flip, which crashes, is
+not, so nothing leads from the one to the other. Charging hops but keeping a crash's turn,
+`pw-flip-hop` stopped jumping at all by 2764, its entropy falling from 22 to 6.7. With the
+bridge both versions of the crash charge flipped from the handover; charging the crash,
+`pw-flip-brc`, landed nearer level than keeping it, `pw-flip-br`, and ships (the table
+below).
+
