+### In the bundle
+
+What the measurements above found missing, and what was done about it:
+
+- **The back flip's tilt fallback, lifted for its mode only.** A manifest mode may now say
+  `"skip_tilt_check": true` (a JSON boolean; anything else is refused, left out it is false), and
+  `scripts/deploy.py` writes `skip_tilt_check = true` into that mode's `[[fsm.state]]` and records
+  it in `bundle.json`'s `built_from` (`compose_fsm`, `manifest_modes`; `deploy/BUNDLE.md`;
+  `tests/test_deploy_hook.py`). Every other mode, and every bundle that does not ask, composes as
+  before. `feedback_stale` still applies with it lifted, and the tick that enters the state is
+  still checked, so a robot already tilted cannot be switched into the flip. What it costs: while
+  the flip runs nothing catches a tilt, so a weak take-off that tips over, or a robot knocked
+  over, keeps running the flip's policy until the mode is left. None of this has run on a
+  physical Jumper.
+- **Both modes in `deploy/crabcraft.manifests.json`**, from locomotion: `power_jumpspin`
+  (`jumper.power_jumpspin720`) on Home + RB and Shift + 6, `power_backflip`
+  (`jumper.power_backflip`, `skip_tilt_check`) on Home + d-pad up and Shift + 7, both let go by
+  `leave_motion` (Menu, Ctrl) like the other moves, with a rule each in
+  `deploy/crabcraft.controller.toml`. Built with `scripts/deploy.py --manifests
+  deploy/crabcraft.manifests.json --allow-incomplete` (the controller at 84b610c01abb; the
+  crate's 183 tests pass, 25 modes, 28 rules); `deploy.py --check-reference` on it: 24 frames,
+  18 inferences, observation worst 0.000e+00, targets worst 2.235e-08, "this host matches the
+  reference". Through that bundle and crabcraft's `jumper_sim.Host`, from standing, 2 trials
+  each, the switch pressed again 1.4 s (720) and 1.2 s (flip) after the handover: the 720 took
+  off 0.22 and 0.225 s after the handover, turned 577 and 608 degrees in the air at 0.24 and
+  0.26 m of rise and was under 8 degrees of tilt 0.3 s after the touchdown; the flip took off at
+  0.16 s, turned 385 and 388 degrees nose-up at 0.20 m, never went to `safe`, and was under 1
+  degree 0.3 s after the touchdown. Both were walking again afterwards.
+- **A way out after the jump.** Neither jump has a recording or a clock, so its mode still ends
+  only on its switch pressed again, or Menu (Ctrl) on its own; any time after the landing will
+  do. The bundle adds no clock of its own. Crabcraft's Robot Mode presses the switch for the
+  person 1.4 s after the 720's handover and 1.2 s after the flip's, and records the game's clips
+  of both moves through this bundle letting go at the same moments. With the settled exports
+  those delays are when each is at rest, not before ("Settling after the landing",
+  "Handed back").
+
+## Settling after the landing
+
+Both shipped jumps shook once they had landed, and nothing in their training asked them
+not to. `tools/eval_calm.py` hands each one over the controller's way (held 0.3 s at
+`HOME`, the deterministic policy from the go to 4 s after the reset, no back-at-`HOME`
+termination, native:cpu) and measures the window from 0.5 s after the landing to the end.
+There, `pw-flip-brc`'s back flip moved its gait joints at 2.98 rad/s RMS and rocked the
+base at 41.3 deg/s RMS in roll and pitch (DR off); some gait joint was over 1.70 N*m in
+90% of the steps, 13.4% of the joint-steps, and its worst joint (RF_J2) swung 2.29 deg
+RMS about its own 0.2 s average with the spectrum's peak at 22.8 Hz -- for the whole 3 s,
+the second difference of the joint angles 0.50 to 0.57 deg a step in every quarter
+second. The 720 swayed less: 1.21 rad/s, 18.4 deg/s, 4.3% of the joint-steps. Under both,
+the actuator force of every one of the 20 joints had content above 10 Hz, 1.0 to 1.1
+N*m RMS on the worst: targets alternating faster than the joint moves, which the joint's
+angle hides and a servo would not.
+
+**Why.** The turn and the apex are latched at the landing and paid every step after it
+whatever the body does, 16 a second for the flip; `action_rate_l2` charged its chatter
+0.11 a second, and `home_pose` pays 1 at most. `back_home` ended an episode 0.1 s after
+the robot first stood at `HOME`, so the 720 was never trained to stay anywhere, and the
+flip, which shook, never got there and spent the rest of every episode shaking with
+nothing against it. Both trained with large action noise, 0.90 to 1.27 for the flip (0.45
+to 0.64 rad of target) and 0.26 to 0.96 for the 720, so what followed a landing in
+training was mostly the noise's.
+
+### What changed
+
+`power_jumpspin.env_cfg.settle_after_landing`, applied to both shipped jumps after the
+handover start (`jumper.power_jumpspin`, which ships nothing, keeps its own config):
+
+- **Four charges and one payment for rest** (`power_jumpspin/mdp/calm.py`), per second:
+
+  | term | what | 720 | flip |
+  |---|---|---|---|
+  | `calm_joint_vel` | the 20 gait joints' summed squared speed, (rad/s)^2 | -0.04 | -0.08 |
+  | `calm_action_rate` | `action_rate_l2`'s summed squared change of the raw action | -0.4 | -0.8 |
+  | `calm_torque` | their actuators' summed squared force, (N*m)^2 | -0.2 | -0.4 |
+  | `calm_base_ang_vel` | the base's squared roll and pitch rate, (rad/s)^2 | -2.0 | -4.0 |
+  | `calm_home` | `exp(-e / 0.05)`, `e` `home_pose`'s mean squared distance from `HOME` | 2.0 | 2.0 |
+
+  The flip's charges are the 720's times `SETTLE_CHARGE_FACTOR`, 2 (`power_backflip/
+  env_cfg.py`): it shook harder, and at the 720's weights 950 iterations left it at 0.47
+  rad/s and 17 deg/s, at twice them at 0.18 and 7.5 (`cm-flip-g`, `cm-flip-g8`, DR on).
+  Each term is gated on the episode **settling** (the command's `settling`, counted in its
+  `settle_steps`): its own jump has landed, or it could have made one and the recording's
+  span has run out without it. The gate is 0 until 0.2 s after that and 1 from 0.5 s, so
+  the catch of the landing is left alone. A reference-state spawn past the bottom of the
+  crouch, which can make no jump of its own, never settles (below).
+- **No `back_home` termination, and 4 s episodes** (`SETTLE_EPISODE_S`): the longest
+  hold, the jump and 2.7 s or more of standing after it.
+
+The actor's observation (406) and the critic's are unchanged, and so is the motor model;
+nothing in `rl/` or `tasks/jumper/common/` changed, and the bundle and controller need
+nothing new. Both shipped lines resumed the shipped checkpoints whole -- actor, critic,
+optimiser -- with the action noise capped at 0.15 first (`tools/cap_noise.py`:
+`std_param.clamp(max=0.15)`), so that the charges start on the policy's
+own shaking rather than on noise of 0.6 rad.
+
+### How it got there
+
+**Charged, it came to rest.** The first runs charged the same four motions from the
+shipped checkpoints at their own noise, gated on `jump_done` (the landing, or the span
+run out, for every episode): -0.01, -0.1, -0.05 and -0.5 (`cm-flip`, `cm-720`) and twice
+that (`-2x`) for 1500 iterations, then four times (`-4x`) and, for the flip, eight
+(`-8x`) for 1000 more. At four times the 720 was at rest (`cm-720-4x` `model_8494`, DR
+on: 0.05 rad/s, 3.7 deg/s), and the flip at eight (`cm-flip-8x` `model_6100`, DR on: 0.08
+rad/s, 3.0 deg/s, no actuator over 1.70 N*m), both landing from the handover as before.
+
+**And learnt to fall.** Half the training episodes spawn along the recording's plain
+jump; those past the bottom of its crouch can make no jump of their own and are paid
+nothing for one, and gated on `jump_done` they were charged for standing once the span
+ran out. A fall ends the charges. In the training's own configuration (64 environments,
+8 s, the policy's own noise, the same seed each time), falls being the tilt and crash
+terminations:
+
+| checkpoint | settling | episodes ended | falls | of them from a spawn that could not jump |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `pw-jspin720-ho` `model_5996` | (none) | 128 | 4 | 2 |
+| `cm-720-4x` `model_8494` | charged, every episode | 159 | 67 | 65 |
+| `cm-720-k` `model_9993` | `cm-720-4x`, then paid | 132 | 24 | 23 |
+| `cm-720-kc` `model_7995` | paid | 128 | 2 | 1 |
+| **`cm-720-g` `model_7250`** | **charged, sparing those spawns** | 128 | **1** | 1 |
+| `pw-flip-brc` `model_3997` | (none) | 128 | 3 | 2 |
+| `cm-flip-8x` `model_6100` | charged, every episode | 143 | 38 | 38 |
+| `cm-flip-k` `model_7599` | `cm-flip-8x`, then paid | 140 | 31 | 30 |
+| **`lg-flip` `model_5900`** | **charged, sparing those spawns** | 128 | **3** | 3 |
+
+Those falls came about 1 s after the go, just before the charges would have begun. From
+the handover the policies that fell there landed as well as the shipped ones (`cm-720-4x`
+32 of 32, `cm-flip-8x` 31 of 32, DR on), but a policy rewarded for giving up after a
+landing it did not make might give up after one it did.
+
+**Paid instead, the flip's noise grew.** `weight * exp(-x / scale)` per motion never
+charges, which removes the reason to fall for every episode. Trained so from the shipped
+720 (`cm-720-kc`, 2000 iterations, the noise capped at 0.15) the 720 came to rest and
+fell no more than before (the table). The flip did not: its noise grew from 0.15 to 0.63
+in 2000 iterations and to 0.76 in 400 more (`cm-flip-kc`, `cm-flip-kcL`), and its shaking
+with it, DR on, to 1.30 rad/s at iteration 5996 and 1.63 at 6400. Once the noise alone
+puts a term near 0 the exponential stops pulling, and nothing holds the noise down.
+Fine-tuned so from the penalty-trained policies (`cm-720-k`, `cm-flip-k`), both stayed at
+rest and the falls only partly went.
+
+**Charged, sparing the spawns that cannot jump** -- what is committed. Every other
+episode is charged once it settles, landed or not, so not jumping does not dodge the
+charges, and where an episode is being paid for its jump, 16 a second and more, standing
+still is worth more than falling. Charged linearly, noise costs in proportion to its
+square, and every charged run brought its noise down.
